<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eastern Bloc</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1947">1947</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Communist Bloc, Soviet Bloc, Socialist Bloc</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eastern Bloc, also called the Communist Bloc or Soviet Bloc, was an unofficial coalition of communist states aligned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, from 1947 to 1991, and opposed to the capitalist Western Bloc. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> it meant the Soviet Union and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> states; in Asia it included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> from 1961. Soviet control was tested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-revolution-of-1956">Hungarian Revolution of 1956</a>, which the Soviet Union suppressed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> of 1968, ended by invasion. The bloc disintegrated after the Polish election of June 1989 and the Pan-European Picnic of 19 August 1989.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The song was translated into languages of the <b>Eastern Bloc</b>, among them German and Hungarian; the Museum of Russian Anthems holds fifteen recordings, including versions in German, Hungarian and Hebrew.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wide-is-my-motherland">Wide Is My Motherland</a></li><li><blockquote>The song celebrates glasnost and the end of the Cold War and speaks of hope at a time of tension, as the Communist-run governments of the <b>Eastern Bloc</b> fell from 1989.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wind-of-change">Wind of Change</a></li></ul></section>
